WEBVTT

NOTE Script captions on the planned 12-second grid. Not word-accurate until the narration is recorded.

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.973
Is this book priced wrong?

00:00:03.023 --> 00:00:08.200
Or are we comparing it with a completely different product?

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.460
I built a pricing case study on three hundred

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.860
and sixty-two fictional textbooks.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.200
The hard part wasn't the average.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.267
It was the traps: misfiled series, ambiguous bundles,

00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:26.200
and a Gleebook that a naive parser reads as an e-book.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.120
Matched like for like,

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.068
fifty-four of sixty groups hold one price.

00:00:32.118 --> 00:00:35.200
Two of the six exceptions were filing errors.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.652
The dataset, method and interactive

00:00:38.702 --> 00:00:40.904
walkthrough are linked in the post.

00:00:40.954 --> 00:00:44.200
Everything is invented, and all of it is reproducible.
